# Example: referencing coding-guidelines from a project

Copy the relevant block into the project's `CLAUDE.md` (or `AGENTS.md`) and
adjust the path. Two ways to reference the repo:

- **Checkout / submodule** (Variants A and C) — clone this repo next to (or
  inside) the project and point at files with `@` imports, so the content is
  loaded automatically. `@path` imports are Claude Code-specific: other agents
  do not expand them and would see literal `@...` lines. A project whose
  guidelines live in `AGENTS.md`, or that other agents also use, MUST use
  Variant B instead.
- **Path reference** (Variant B) — name the files and let the agent read them
  on demand. Works with any agent. Prefer this when you want to keep the
  always-on context small.

`@`-imported files always load in full: the `paths:` frontmatter on this
repo's language files is honoured only for files under `.claude/rules/`, not
for `@` imports.

Ref: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory

Where the owner's dotfiles already link the guidelines into
`~/.claude/rules/` (see README, "Auto-loading in Claude Code"), skip
`principles.md`, the `languages/` files, `frontend/common.md` and
`frontend/svelte.md`: they load on their own. Import only `frontend/react.md`
or `frontend/solid.md` and the `patterns/` files the project needs.

---

## Variant A: Go service, guidelines checked out as a sibling directory

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

This project follows ../coding-guidelines. Load before writing code:

@../coding-guidelines/principles.md
@../coding-guidelines/languages/go.md
```

This import points outside the project, so Claude Code shows a one-time
approval dialog on the first session; declining disables the imports and the
dialog does not reappear.

## Variant B: React + TypeScript app, loaded on demand

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

This project follows the rules in ~/engineer/coding-guidelines.
Before writing or reviewing code, read:

- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/principles.md   (always)
- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/languages/typescript.md
- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/frontend/common.md
- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/frontend/react.md

For DB, HTTP, or codegen work, additionally load the matching
~/engineer/coding-guidelines/patterns/ file (persistence / http / codegen /
tooling).

Rules there are normative (MUST/SHOULD/NEVER). If a project-local rule in
this file conflicts with the guidelines, this file wins — note the conflict
in your response.
```

## Variant C: git submodule pinned to a commit

```bash
git submodule add https://example.com/you/coding-guidelines docs/guidelines
```

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

@docs/guidelines/principles.md
@docs/guidelines/languages/rust.md
```

Pin the submodule and bump it deliberately, so guideline changes arrive as
reviewable diffs instead of silently changing agent behavior.

## Bootstrapping tooling (any variant)

Per `patterns/tooling.md`, lint/format/pre-commit is mandatory: copy
`templates/lefthook.yaml.example` → `lefthook.yaml` and
`templates/gitleaks.toml.example` → `.gitleaks.toml` at the project root,
then run `lefthook install`. Also copy or paste (per each template's own
header) the config template(s) for the project's stack:
`golangci.yaml.example`; `cargo-lints.toml.example` + `clippy.toml.example` +
`rustfmt.toml.example` + `deny.toml.example`; `oxlintrc.json.example` + `oxfmtrc.json.example` +
`tsconfig.json.example`, plus `oxlintrc.react.json.example` for React, or
`eslint.svelte.config.example` / `eslint.solid.config.example` for
Svelte/Solid.
